    [
        'id'=>'ce-applied-methods-layout-and-delivery','board'=>'civil-engineering','subject'=>'ce-applied','title'=>'Applied Civil Engineering: Models, Surveying, Transport, and Construction','pdf'=>'ce-applied-methods-layout-and-delivery.pdf',
        'scope'=>['prc-cele-program-march-2026'],'instruction'=>['openstax-calculus-volume-1','nist-semtech-engineering-statistics-handbook','noaa-geodetic-leveling-manual','fhwa-traffic-flow-theory','fhwa-construction-program-management-inspection','fhwa-field-materials-manual','mit-structural-engineering-design-notes'],
        'scope_note'=>'The March 2026 PRC CELE program identifies applied mathematics, engineering data, surveying and layout, transportation/highways, quantity surveying, construction safety, and construction management. This original guide teaches selected shared methods and is not a complete CE reviewer or a substitute for current project specifications.',
        'objectives'=>['Translate an engineering situation into a dimensional model and check the result.','Explain how field observations, quantities, and assumptions affect a construction decision.','Use a transparent comparison before recommending a transport or project action.'],
        'sections'=>[
            ['id'=>'models-units-and-data','heading'=>'1. Models, units, and engineering data','body'=>'A quantitative result is meaningful only when the model matches the question and every quantity has compatible units. Start by sketching the physical situation, naming knowns and unknowns, and writing the governing relationship before substituting numbers. Convert units before arithmetic, carry units through intermediate steps, and estimate the order of magnitude. Engineering data also need context: sample size, measurement method, spread, and the conditions under which observations were collected. A precise calculation based on a wrong model or unrepresentative data is still a poor decision.','concepts'=>['b633-ce-applied-mathematics','b633-ce-engineering-data']],
            ['id'=>'surveying-quantity-and-layout','heading'=>'2. Surveying, layout, and quantities','body'=>'Surveying turns a reference system and field observations into positions, elevations, distances, and layout decisions. A level loop should close against a known benchmark or another defensible check; a closure difference is evidence to investigate, not a value to hide by arbitrary adjustment. For quantity work, define the measurement boundary, units, drawing revision, and assumptions before multiplying dimensions. Reconcile field conditions with the controlled plans and record the source of each quantity. Measurement uncertainty, waste allowances, and pay-item rules must be distinguished rather than blended into one unexplained factor.','concepts'=>['b633-ce-surveying-layout','b633-ce-construction-quantity-productivity']],
            ['id'=>'transport-and-safe-construction','heading'=>'3. Transportation and construction decisions','body'=>'Transportation questions connect movement, capacity, travel time, safety, and land use. Compare alternatives with the same time period and denominator: a count is not a rate, and an average can conceal variation by location or time. Construction planning similarly aligns work sequence, labor and equipment, materials, inspection points, and hazards. Identify the activity, exposure, and people affected before choosing a control; do not treat a checklist as proof that a hazard is controlled. Current law, contract requirements, and site conditions govern actual work.','concepts'=>['b633-ce-transportation-highway','b633-ce-construction-safety-quality']],
            ['id'=>'economics-and-schedule-review','heading'=>'4. Cost, schedule, and a defensible recommendation','body'=>'A project comparison should state the alternatives, service period, included costs, assumptions, and any constraints that change the result. Present value, equivalent worth, or a simple payback comparison can support a decision, but none removes uncertainty about demand, maintenance, or delivery risk. A schedule is a network of precedence relationships and durations; a critical path identifies activities whose delay can move the completion date under the modeled logic. Update assumptions when actual progress differs, preserve the reason for a change, and avoid reporting a forecast as a guaranteed finish date.','concepts'=>['b633-ce-engineering-economics-scheduling']],
        ],
        'examples'=>[['heading'=>'Check a dimensional model','prompt'=>'A simply supported beam has span 4 m, center load 8 kN, E = 200 GPa, and I = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ m⁴. Using the supplied relation δ = PL³/(48EI), find midspan deflection.','steps'=>['Convert P to 8,000 N and E to 200 × 10⁹ N/m²; keep L in metres and I in m⁴.','Substitute: δ = 8,000(4³)/(48 × 200 × 10⁹ × 3.0 × 10⁻⁵) = 0.00178 m.','Convert metres to millimetres by multiplying by 1,000.'],'answer'=>'Approximately 1.78 mm under the stated idealized model.','check'=>'The dimensional reduction is metres; this is a model result, not a design acceptance check.']],
        'misconceptions'=>['Unit cancellation is optional in a calculation.','Survey closure error should be hidden rather than investigated and reported.','A critical-path forecast guarantees project completion on that date.'],
